<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>An illustrative example of the heterogeneous graph structure used in our framework.
The graph consists of three types of nodes: <span id="A5.F9.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">question nodes</span> (green, top), representing input questions;
<span id="A5.F9.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">agent nodes</span> (orange, middle), corresponding to different reasoning or retrieval agents;
and <span id="A5.F9.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">entity nodes</span> (blue, bottom), representing extracted knowledge items such as people, films, time expressions, or attributes.
Edges capture various types of relations between these nodes: dashed gray edges denote linguistic or semantic relations between entities,
teal edges indicate <span id="A5.F9.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">manage</span> links connecting agents to the entities they are responsible for,
purple edges represent <span id="A5.F9.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">q2agent</span> connections from the question to potentially useful agents,
and red edges (<span id="A5.F9.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">q_ref</span>) link questions directly to referenced entities.</figcaption>
</figure>
